        "notes": "Pass 1 (author sourcing, 2026-10-14): both pages fetched; each supports 'traditional game' (MCST lists it under 전통놀이; the encyclopedia files it under 놀이), the weighted core wrapped in paper or cloth with a fringe, and kicking to keep it up for the highest count. Seasonal timing (New Year in the encyclopedia, winter on the MCST page) deliberately left out of the note.\nPass 2 (independent fact-check, 2026-10-14): re-opened both pages. Encyclopedia 정의 gives kicking the jegi without letting it drop and competing on the count, and 내용 paragraph 1 gives a coin wrapped in hanji or silk with the ends torn into fringes; MCST 놀이방법 gives repeated kicks so it does not touch the ground, higher count wins, and lists it under 전통놀이. Every clause of the note is supported; 'weighted' follows from the coin or metal core. Note is an English paraphrase with no wording copied from either page. Outcome: pass, no change.\nPass 5 (MAR culture review, 2026-10-14): re-checked against both sources (both HTTP 200). Encyclopedia 정의 and 내용 paragraph 1 and MCST 놀이방법 support every clause: a traditional game, a coin or metal core wrapped in hanji, silk, paper or cloth with a torn fringe, kicked to keep it off the ground, highest count wins. Both sources treat the jegi itself as the toy the game uses, which backs the q2 Hoya line 'Jegi and spinning tops are traditional Korean toys'. No copied wording, no designation number, no person, shop, brand or price. Outcome: pass, no change to the note."
